# Git flow (GitHub): feature branch → develop → main

GitHub rulesets **`protect-develop`** and **`protect-main`** block direct pushes (same pattern as [gghstats](https://github.com/hrodrig/gghstats)).

## Day-to-day

1. Update local `develop` (`git fetch origin && git switch develop && git pull`).
2. Create a **local branch** (`feat/…`, `fix/…`, `chore/…`).
3. Commit on that branch. **Never** `git push origin develop` or `git push origin main`.
4. Push the feature branch and open a **PR with base `develop`**.
5. Merge on GitHub after required CI checks pass.

## Releases

1. Integration is already on `develop`. All tests pass (`make test` / `make release-check` before tag).
2. Open a PR **`develop` → `main`**. Do not push `main`.
3. After merge, on `main`: annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and `git push origin vX.Y.Z` (GoReleaser) when releasing.
4. **Sync `main` back into `develop`** (see below). Do this even for docs-only `develop` → `main` merges.

## After `develop` → `main` (required sync)

Merge commits on `main` are **not** on `develop`. GitHub then shows **"This branch is out-of-date with the base branch"** on the next `develop` → `main` PR (strict status checks / outdated branch).

**Fix — always after merging into `main`:**

1. Branch from `develop`: `git fetch origin && git switch develop && git pull && git switch -c chore/sync-main-into-develop`
2. `git merge origin/main` (or open PR **`main` → `develop`**)
3. Push and open a PR **into `develop`**. Merge when CI is green.

Do **not** force-push `develop` or `main`. Do **not** push either protected branch directly.

## Never

- Direct push to `develop` or `main`
- Feature commits on `main`
- Leaving `develop` behind `main` after a release (or docs) merge
- Invent a remote or `git push` unless the operator asked
